    "details": {
      "biography": {
        "value": "<p><strong>Look.</strong> Eldest son, 30s. Flushed face, black cloak with the collar hitched over his head, grinning papier-mache pumpkin head under one arm. Usually holding a glass.</p><p><strong>Wants.</strong> His inheritance, to pay debts that terrify him. Underneath, his father's approval.</p><p><strong>Secret.</strong> Ruinous gambling debts. Letter H3 in his coat says a new creditor bought them all; sealed in green wax with a mortar-and-pestle stamp. He spent 8:30-9:00 alone in the wine cellar (true).</p><p><strong>Knows.</strong> Around 8:40 he heard footsteps and dripping behind the cellar's bricked-up arch. Goes pale when he learns Quill's signet ring bears a mortar and pestle. Quill frames him at candle 8.</p><p><strong>Plays as.</strong> Bluster, self-pity and bad jokes.</p><p><strong>Lines.</strong></p><ul><li>“I'd say I lost my head, but that's just the costume.”</li><li>“Father and I argued about money. We always argued about money. That's not murder, that's family.”</li></ul>",
        "public": ""
      },
      "alignment": "Chaotic Neutral",
